      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor a spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, with each component in\n$\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and the Euclidean norm, define\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}$, with\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here $\\omega_\\mu$ is the temporal wave number, not the\nSI angular frequency; the corresponding SI angular frequency is\n$c\\omega_\\mu$. Energy and momentum are $E=\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ in joules and\n$\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With\n$p=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4$, the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nFor a selected local action represented on a chart region\n$\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ by\n$$\\frac{S}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathcal U}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}(x)\\,d^4x,\\quad d^4x:=dx^0dx^1dx^2dx^3,$$\nthe natural Lagrangian density $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ has unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-4}$. Since $x^0=ct$, the corresponding SI Lagrangian density is\n$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\hbar c\\,\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ and has unit\n$\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. This unit conversion alone does not identify a\nLagrangian density with the model's Hamiltonian energy density.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nuse the formal field symbols $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ and $\\psi_{\\rm n}$ in the selected\nscalar and Dirac kinetic terms, assigning them length degrees $-1$ and\n$-3/2$, respectively. These are the dimensions fixed by those free kinetic\nterms, not exact scaling dimensions of interacting fields: anomalous\ndimensions, when present, are separate quantities. These symbols do not\nassert pointwise field maps or operator fields; a model supplies its field\nspace and regularity. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nIn a supplied four-component spinor representation, let\n$\\gamma^a\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathbb C^4)$, $a=0,1,2,3$, be\ndimensionless linear maps satisfying\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}:=\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a\n=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$, where $I_4$ is the identity map. Set $\\beta:=\\gamma^0$\nand $\\alpha^i:=\\beta\\gamma^i$; then $\\beta^2=I_4$ and\n$\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$. For a supplied four-component spinor $\\psi$ write\n$\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. The effective Clifford metric is $-\\eta$.\nA supplied free Dirac model may use\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$ for its model-supplied spinor field\n$\\psi$; that model supplies its field domain and regularity. The phase and\nClifford choices are conventions; this item proves no representation or field\nequation.\n\nIf a selected model supplies a complex Hilbert space $\\mathscr H$ as in\n[[def-hilbert-space]], a complex-linear Hamiltonian operator\n$H:D(H)\\to\\mathscr H$ with model-specified linear domain $D(H)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$\nand SI energy unit joules, and an evolution family $U(t):\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$\nfor $t\\in I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ in seconds, the convention is\n$U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$. Each $U(t)$ is a surjective complex-linear map\nsatisfying $\\langle U(t)\\xi,U(t)\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,\\zeta\\rangle$\nfor all $\\xi,\\zeta\\in\\mathscr H$; write $U(t)^*:=U(t)^{-1}$. The exponent\nis dimensionless because $\\hbar$ has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. For any\nmodel-supplied complex-linear operator $O:D(O)\\to\\mathscr H$, with specified\nlinear domain $D(O)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$, define its Heisenberg conjugate by\n$$O_H(t):=U(t)^*O\\,U(t),\\quad D(O_H(t)):=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathscr H:U(t)\\xi\\in D(O)\\}.$$\nThe model supplies these domains and the evolution; this dictionary constructs\nno Hamiltonian, evolution family, or field representation. Where a free-field\nmodule uses an inner product linear in its second variable, convert to the\nrepository's first-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction. It\nasserts only matrix-element continuity and the stated linear maps; all further\noperator properties, locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior must be supplied by a constructed\nmodel. Test-function units can be chosen so a specified smeared observable is\ndimensionless; there is no universal test-function unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-qft-free-field-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Fix four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, an inertial length-coordinate chart\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, $x^0=ct$, and coordinates measured in metres, as specified\nby [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Write\n$\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. The mass-shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same unit. For a\nmassive species, $\\mu=m_{\\mathrm{rest,SI}}c/\\hbar$; $\\mu=0$ is the separately\nadmitted massless branch of the mathematical free-field construction. Thus\n$\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ is physical energy and $\\hbar\\mathbf k$ is physical\nmomentum. The natural-unit action is written $S/\\hbar$.\n\nThe scalar one-particle space is\n$\\mathcal H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C)$, where\n$H_\\mu^+=\\{(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k):\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0\\}$ and\n$d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))$; at $\\mu=0$ the\nmeasure-zero apex $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted. These are the shell conventions\nof [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]].\n\nFor a smooth real classical scalar $\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$\nwith the canonical free-field length degree $-1$, adopt\n$$\\frac{S_\\phi}{\\hbar}=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi_{\\mathrm n}\\partial_b\\phi_{\\mathrm n}+\\mu^2\\phi_{\\mathrm n}^2\\right)\\,d^4x.$$\nFor a smooth commuting classical Dirac spinor\n$\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ with canonical free-field length\ndegree $-3/2$, put $\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=\\psi_{\\mathrm n}^\\dagger\\beta$ and\nuse the Clifford convention\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Adopt\n$$\\frac{S_D}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe smooth classical variables in these functionals commute; no Grassmann\nalgebra or path-integral construction is being adopted. These formulas specify\nlocal action densities: for arbitrary smooth global fields the total\nintegrals need not converge. Only compactly supported variations are used;\nthe action difference under such a variation is an integral over a compact\nset and is finite for smooth fields. With these variations on the fixed\nbackground, the intended Euler–Lagrange equations are\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0$, conditional on the exact\nmathematical supplier recorded as [L1] below.\n\nAs the physical interpretation of this selected free model, assign the real\nscalar species to the bosonic symmetric Fock representation and assign the\nDirac field to the particle/antiparticle CAR representation on\n$\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. Choose the zero-particle Fock\nvectors as the scalar and Dirac vacuum states. This is an adopted species and\nstatistics assignment. The CCR/CAR and operator properties of the specified\nrepresentations are mathematical model consequences; they do not prove a\ngeneral spin-statistics theorem or establish that nature realizes this model.\n\nThe scalar construction is the Schwartz-smeared field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, with the future-shell phase\n$p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, exactly as in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. The Dirac construction is the bounded\nspacetime-smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], using the positive and negative\nprojections of the specified free Dirac matrix. In that carrier,\n$\\mathcal H_D=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$, and\n$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=(I_4\\pm h_D(\\mathbf k)/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))/2$; the\nparticle and antiparticle one-particle spaces are the closed ranges\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. These carriers state their\nmathematical hypotheses and construction domains; their draft status is not an\nindependent physics or proof approval.\n\nTong's lecture notes and Srednicki's book draft provide independent\npresentations of the scalar and Dirac free-field formulations. Their formal\nmode notation supplies context for this adoption, not a proof of the\noperator-domain claims and not evidence that the adopted model describes\nnature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the following physical reading only for an explicitly selected model\nwhose mathematical data have actually been constructed. Let $\\mathscr H$ be a\nnonzero complex Hilbert space, let $\\rho_t$ be the model's density operator at\na time $t$ at which a measurement is made, and let\n$\\{M_{a,r}:r=1,\\ldots,q_a\\}_{a=1}^m$ be a supplied normalized finite bounded\nKraus instrument on $\\mathscr H$ in the exact sense of\n[[def-qft-finite-bounded-kraus-instrument]]. Assume the Axiom of Countable\nChoice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ when using the normalization result of\n[[lem-qft-finite-instrument-normalization]]. Its hypotheses include that\n$\\rho_t$ is a density operator and that the instrument really satisfies\n$\\sum_{a,r}M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}=I_{\\mathscr H}$; the supplier's result is a draft\nwith a complete conditional proof, not an independent approval or an\napparatus-realization theorem.\n\nPut\n\n$$E_a:=\\sum_rM_{a,r}^*M_{a,r},\\qquad \\mathcal J_a(X):=\\sum_rM_{a,r}XM_{a,r}^*.$$\n\nAs the **Born interpretation postulate** for this selected model, read\n\n$$p_a(t):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathcal J_a(\\rho_t)) =\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_tE_a)$$\n\nas the model probability for outcome $a$. The mathematical supplier proves\n$p_a(t)\\in[0,1]$ and $\\sum_{a=1}^mp_a(t)=1$ under the hypotheses just\nlisted. When $p_a(t)>0$, adopt\n$\\mathcal J_a(\\rho_t)/p_a(t)$ as the model's conditional state after outcome\n$a$; at zero probability no normalized update is assigned. These are\nmodel-level probability assignments, not observations.\n\nIf the selected construction supplies a self-adjoint observable in the sense\nof [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]] and its\nspectral projection-valued measure as in\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]], a declared finite Borel partition may be\nused only after its projections have been constructed and verified as a\nfinite projective instrument. In that case the projections are the single\nKraus operators for their outcomes. A supplied bounded effect is read as an\noutcome effect only as part of a supplied normalized instrument; this item\ndoes not construct square roots, a spectral measure, or an instrument from an\narbitrary formal observable.\n\nIf a selected construction supplies a self-adjoint Hamiltonian\n$\\widehat H:D(\\widehat H)\\subseteq\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$ in the sense of\n[[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]], and an evolution\nfamily $U(t):\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$ whose maps are surjective\ncomplex-linear and preserve the Hilbert inner product, as specified in\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], for $t\\in I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ (with $t$ in\nseconds), adopt the Schrödinger convention\n$U(t)=\\exp(-it\\widehat H/\\hbar)$ and\n$\\rho_t=U(t)\\rho_0U(t)^*$ when that construction establishes that $\\rho_t$\nis a density operator. The unitary convention and SI dimensions are those of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], where $U(t)^*:=U(t)^{-1}$ and $\\widehat H$ has\nunit joules. For any supplied complex-linear operator\n$O:D(O)\\to\\mathscr H$ with specified linear domain $D(O)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$,\ndefine its Heisenberg conjugate by\n$$O_H(t):=U(t)^*O\\,U(t),\\quad D(O_H(t)):=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathscr H:U(t)\\xi\\in D(O)\\}.$$\nThe model supplies these domains and the evolution. This postulate neither\nconstructs the Hamiltonian, evolution family, state, or field representation,\nnor any model-specific operator domain.\n\nThe model declaration identifies the selected species, field normalization,\nregulator, preparation and measurement data using\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. It does not assert that every classical action\nhas a quantization, that every formal state is positive, or that any cutoff\ncan be removed. In particular it supplies no interacting continuum\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space, scattering operator, asymptotic\ncompleteness, LSZ limit, or nonperturbative convergence. A formal coefficient\nis not an exact probability without an additional controlled approximation\nmap and a constructed model. Agreement with reported data would test the\nselected approximation and empirical inputs; it would not prove this\npostulate or the model. No reported data are asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qft-free-field-observables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the fields, Fock spaces, vacua, coordinate conventions, and natural/SI\nnormalizations adopted in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. For an open spacetime\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, a scalar test is a real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)$ and a Dirac test is a\n$F\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$. These are compactly supported smooth test\nfunctions on the fixed inertial Minkowski chart.\n\nFor scalar tests use the bounded Weyl operators\n$$W_\\mu(f)=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere $K_\\mu$ is the future-shell restriction and\n$W_{\\rm Segal}$ is the unitary generated by the closed Segal field, with the\nnormalization and phase convention of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the scalar bounded observable algebra\n$\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ to be the norm closure of the unital $*$-algebra generated\nby these $W_\\mu(f)$. The real smeared field is symmetric on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its self-adjoint closure has the exact multiplier\ndomain supplied by [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] and\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]].\n\nFor Dirac tests, $\\Psi(F)$ is the bounded smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]]. Define $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ as the norm\nclosure of the unital algebra generated by products containing an even number\nof these field operators and their adjoints, with all test supports in $O$.\n“Even” here means even field parity, or equivalently an even number of odd\nCAR field generators in each product. The even algebra is the selected local\nDirac observable algebra; an odd charged field is not included as a commuting\nlocal observable.\n\nThe selected vacuum states on bounded operators are the vector states\n$\\omega_s(A)=\\langle\\Omega_s,A\\Omega_s\\rangle$ and\n$\\omega_D(B)=\\langle\\Omega_D,B\\Omega_D\\rangle$. For the scalar Fock space,\ndefine the total number, Hamiltonian, and physical momentum components by\n$$N_s=d\\Gamma_s(1),\\qquad H_s=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma_s(\\omega_\\mu),\\qquad P_{s,j}=\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma_s(k_j).$$\nHere $d\\Gamma_s$ acts on the $n$-particle sector by summing the displayed\none-particle multiplier over its $n$ arguments. Their maximal domains are\nthe square-integrability domains of those sector sums; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThese physical components have one-particle momentum $+\\hbar k_j$, as in\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. The mathematical spatial generators in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] use\n$G_{s,j}=d\\Gamma_s(-k_j)$ with the positive-exponent convention;\nthus $P_{s,j}=-\\hbar G_{s,j}$. For a translation increment\n$a=(a^0,\\mathbf a)$ in length coordinates, the scalar translation unitary\nis the sectorwise phase\n$\\exp(i(a^0H_s/(\\hbar c)-\\sum_j a^jP_{s,j}/\\hbar))$.\nChanging the sign does not change the maximal domain, since the squared\nsector multiplier is unchanged. This also follows directly from\n[[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]] with multiplier $k_j$. For the Dirac Fock space, let $H$\nand $P_j$ be the mathematical translation generators of\n[[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. Their one-particle\nmultipliers are respectively $(\\omega_\\mu,\\mathbf k)$ for particles and\n$(\\omega_\\mu,-\\mathbf k)$ for antiparticles, and the sector multiplier is\ntheir sum. Define the physical Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum by\n$H_D=\\hbar cH$ and $P_{D,j}=\\hbar P_j$, using the positive SI constants from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Their exact domains are unchanged by these\nnonzero scale factors; their multipliers have units joules and momentum,\nrespectively.\n\nFor any of these self-adjoint operators $A$, a normalized state\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$.\nThis requires the second spectral moment but does not require\n$\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. Products of unbounded operators use their composition\ndomains.\n\nIf a signed charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in coulombs is supplied, the intended\nDirac charge-number operator is $Q_{\\rm SI}=q_{\\rm SI}(N_p-N_{\\bar p})$.\nHere $\\Pi_p$ and $\\Pi_{\\bar p}$ are the orthogonal projections onto\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$; on the $n$-particle sector,\n$N_p$ and $N_{\\bar p}$ are the sums of the corresponding projections over\nthe $n$ tensor factors. Its candidate maximal direct-sum domain is\n$$D(Q_{\\rm SI})=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left\\|q_{\\rm SI}\\sum_{r=1}^n(\\Pi_p-\\Pi_{\\bar p})^{(r)}\\Psi_n\\right\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThis is a candidate definition only; no self-adjointness or proved-domain\nclaim is asserted here.\n\nMeasurements in this model are assigned only when a state and a normalized\nfinite bounded Kraus instrument have actually been constructed. For such an\ninstrument, the adopted outcome probabilities and positive-probability\nconditional states are exactly those of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]. They are model probabilities,\nnot observed outcomes. The field definitions are test-smeared: neither\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$ nor $\\Psi(x)$ is a pointwise operator supplied here, and the\nsmearing results do not define coincident products or a diagonal restriction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qft-free-field-observables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the fields, Fock spaces, vacua, coordinate conventions, and natural/SI\nnormalizations adopted in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. For an open spacetime\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, a scalar test is a real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)$ and a Dirac test is a\n$F\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$. These are compactly supported smooth test\nfunctions on the fixed inertial Minkowski chart.\n\nFor scalar tests use the bounded Weyl operators\n$$W_\\mu(f)=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere $K_\\mu$ is the future-shell restriction and\n$W_{\\rm Segal}$ is the unitary generated by the closed Segal field, with the\nnormalization and phase convention of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the scalar bounded observable algebra\n$\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ to be the norm closure of the unital $*$-algebra generated\nby these $W_\\mu(f)$. The real smeared field is symmetric on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its self-adjoint closure has the exact multiplier\ndomain supplied by [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] and\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]].\n\nFor Dirac tests, $\\Psi(F)$ is the bounded smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]]. Define $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ as the norm\nclosure of the unital algebra generated by products containing an even number\nof these field operators and their adjoints, with all test supports in $O$.\n“Even” here means even field parity, or equivalently an even number of odd\nCAR field generators in each product. The even algebra is the selected local\nDirac observable algebra; an odd charged field is not included as a commuting\nlocal observable.\n\nThe selected vacuum states on bounded operators are the vector states\n$\\omega_s(A)=\\langle\\Omega_s,A\\Omega_s\\rangle$ and\n$\\omega_D(B)=\\langle\\Omega_D,B\\Omega_D\\rangle$. For the scalar Fock space,\ndefine the total number, Hamiltonian, and physical momentum components by\n$$N_s=d\\Gamma_s(1),\\qquad H_s=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma_s(\\omega_\\mu),\\qquad P_{s,j}=\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma_s(k_j).$$\nHere $d\\Gamma_s$ acts on the $n$-particle sector by summing the displayed\none-particle multiplier over its $n$ arguments. Their maximal domains are\nthe square-integrability domains of those sector sums; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThese physical components have one-particle momentum $+\\hbar k_j$, as in\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. The mathematical spatial generators in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] use\n$G_{s,j}=d\\Gamma_s(-k_j)$ with the positive-exponent convention;\nthus $P_{s,j}=-\\hbar G_{s,j}$. For a translation increment\n$a=(a^0,\\mathbf a)$ in length coordinates, the scalar translation unitary\nis the sectorwise phase\n$\\exp(i(a^0H_s/(\\hbar c)-\\sum_j a^jP_{s,j}/\\hbar))$.\nChanging the sign does not change the maximal domain, since the squared\nsector multiplier is unchanged. This also follows directly from\n[[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]] with multiplier $k_j$. For the Dirac Fock space, let $H$\nand $P_j$ be the mathematical translation generators of\n[[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. Their one-particle\nmultipliers are respectively $(\\omega_\\mu,\\mathbf k)$ for particles and\n$(\\omega_\\mu,-\\mathbf k)$ for antiparticles, and the sector multiplier is\ntheir sum. Define the physical Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum by\n$H_D=\\hbar cH$ and $P_{D,j}=\\hbar P_j$, using the positive SI constants from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Their exact domains are unchanged by these\nnonzero scale factors; their multipliers have units joules and momentum,\nrespectively.\n\nFor any of these self-adjoint operators $A$, a normalized state\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$.\nThis requires the second spectral moment but does not require\n$\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. Products of unbounded operators use their composition\ndomains.\n\nIf a signed charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in coulombs is supplied, the intended\nDirac charge-number operator is $Q_{\\rm SI}=q_{\\rm SI}(N_p-N_{\\bar p})$.\nHere $\\Pi_p$ and $\\Pi_{\\bar p}$ are the orthogonal projections onto\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$; on the $n$-particle sector,\n$N_p$ and $N_{\\bar p}$ are the sums of the corresponding projections over\nthe $n$ tensor factors. Its candidate maximal direct-sum domain is\n$$D(Q_{\\rm SI})=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left\\|q_{\\rm SI}\\sum_{r=1}^n(\\Pi_p-\\Pi_{\\bar p})^{(r)}\\Psi_n\\right\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThis is a candidate definition only; no self-adjointness or proved-domain\nclaim is asserted here.\n\nMeasurements in this model are assigned only when a state and a normalized\nfinite bounded Kraus instrument have actually been constructed. For such an\ninstrument, the adopted outcome probabilities and positive-probability\nconditional states are exactly those of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]. They are model probabilities,\nnot observed outcomes. The field definitions are test-smeared: neither\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$ nor $\\Psi(x)$ is a pointwise operator supplied here, and the\nsmearing results do not define coincident products or a diagonal restriction.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and use the scalar and Dirac free-field constructions of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]] with their current draft hypotheses, including the Dirac projection and CAR inputs in [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For a real scalar test $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)$ is self-adjoint on the exact domain given by [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. On scalar bosonic Fock space, $N_s=d\\Gamma(1)$, $H_s=d\\Gamma(\\omega_\\mu)$, and $P_{s,j}=d\\Gamma(-k_j)$ are the maximal real multipliers of [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nand the Hamiltonian and momentum domains are exactly the square-integrability domains of the sums of their one-particle multipliers.\n\nOn the specified Dirac Fock space, spacetime-smeared fields $\\Psi(F)$ are bounded, while the translation generators $H_D,P_{D,j}$ have the exact maximal domains and joint PVM stated in [[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. For any one of these self-adjoint real-multiplier operators $A$, a normalized $\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has finite mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and finite variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2.$$\nMembership in $D(A)$ suffices for finite variance and does not require $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. A product $AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$, as specified in [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]].\n\nFor the scalar point limit, fix $x_0\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and real tests $f_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $0<\\varepsilon\\le1$, such that $\\int f_\\varepsilon(x)\\phi(x)\\,d^4x\\to\\phi(x_0)$ for every Schwartz test $\\phi$ and $K_\\mu f_\\varepsilon(p)\\to e^{ip\\circ x_0}$ for $\\nu_\\mu$-almost every shell momentum. Then $\\|\\Phi_\\mu(f_\\varepsilon)\\Omega\\|\\to\\infty$. A sufficient condition for both convergences is $\\int f_\\varepsilon=1$, $\\sup_\\varepsilon\\|f_\\varepsilon\\|_1<\\infty$, and $\\int_{|x-x_0|\\ge\\delta}|f_\\varepsilon(x)|\\,d^4x\\to0$ for each $\\delta>0$. This includes all nonnegative normalized Schwartz approximate identities with mass concentrating at $x_0$, as well as signed families satisfying that condition; weak distributional convergence alone is not substituted for convergence of the mass-shell Fourier restriction. For the equal-time Dirac CAR field, fix $\\mathbf x_0\\in\\mathbb R^3$ and $z_0\\in\\mathbb C^4\\setminus\\{0\\}$. For every family $u_\\varepsilon\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ satisfying $\\int h(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger u_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\to h(\\mathbf x_0)^\\dagger z_0$ for each spinor Schwartz test $h$, the bounded equal-time smeared operators satisfy $\\|\\Psi_t(u_\\varepsilon)\\|_{\\rm op}\\to\\infty$ at each fixed time $t$. Thus neither construction gives a point-field value in the indicated norm topology. The supplied free-field results define only smeared fields and do not define coincident diagonal products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].\nLet $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define\n$$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere the Segal normalization is exactly\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the self-adjoint generator\nof $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the closure $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}$,\nwhose restriction to $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is the real scalar field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator\nalgebra\n$$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$\nThen $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference\n$x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed\nrepresentation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. Define\n$$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4):=\\{F=(F_0,F_1,F_2,F_3):F_a\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)\\text{ for }a=0,1,2,3\\},$$\nwith the finite-product topology generated by the scalar Schwartz seminorms on each component; $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and its seminorms are those of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. For $F$ in this space, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. Define\n$$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4):=\\{F=(F_0,F_1,F_2,F_3):F_a\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)\\text{ for }a=0,1,2,3\\},$$\nwith the finite-product topology generated by the scalar Schwartz seminorms on each component; $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and its seminorms are those of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. For $F$ in this space, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "1.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-countable-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-countable-choice.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematical carrier inspected; this excerpt records the exact assumption and is not independent approval.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition supplies vocabulary; no theorem proof is claimed.",
          "consumer_use": "A1 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-framework-units.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; no current Step 1 receipt was found for batch 40, whose owner status requires repair/recertification.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ put\n$\\omega(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here\n$\\omega$ is the temporal wave number, not the SI angular frequency; the\ncorresponding SI angular frequency is $c\\omega$. Energy and momentum are\n$E=\\hbar c\\omega$ in joules and $\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in\n$\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$ the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nThe natural action is $S/\\hbar$. Its canonical density in four dimensions has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-4}$; the corresponding physical energy density is\n$\\hbar c$ times that density and has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nwrite $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ for a scalar field with length degree $-1$ and\n$\\psi_{\\rm n}$ for a Dirac field with length degree $-3/2$. These are the\ndimensions fixed by the selected free kinetic terms, not exact scaling\ndimensions of interacting fields: anomalous dimensions, when present, are\nseparate quantities. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad\nf^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nThe Clifford convention is $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$,\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}$, so the effective Clifford metric is\n$-\\eta$. In a supplied free Dirac model the displayed equation is\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$. The phase and Clifford choices are\nconventions; this item proves no Dirac representation or field equation.\nTime evolution is written $U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$ when a selected model has\nactually constructed a self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ and its unitary group; the\ncorresponding Heisenberg conjugation is $O_H(t)=U(t)^*O\\,U(t)$. No such\nHamiltonian or group is constructed here. Where a free-field module uses an\ninner product linear in its second variable, convert to the repository's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction.\nSmeared fields are not thereby bounded, symmetric, self-adjoint, or\nself-adjointly closable. Locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior are likewise supplied only by the\nrelevant constructed model modules. Test-function units can be chosen so a\nspecified smeared observable is dimensionless; there is no universal test\nfunction unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition supplies conventions only; it does not prove a field equation.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-qft-free-field-model",
          "carrier": "items/post-qft-free-field-model.md",
          "status": "Current draft postulate inspected; no current Step 1 receipt exists. Its mathematical field/action inputs are conditional.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Fix four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, an inertial length-coordinate chart\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, $x^0=ct$, and coordinates measured in metres, as specified\nby [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Write\n$\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. The mass-shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same unit. For a\nmassive species, $\\mu=m_{\\mathrm{rest,SI}}c/\\hbar$; $\\mu=0$ is the separately\nadmitted massless branch of the mathematical free-field construction. Thus\n$\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ is physical energy and $\\hbar\\mathbf k$ is physical\nmomentum. The natural-unit action is written $S/\\hbar$.\n\nThe scalar one-particle space is\n$\\mathcal H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C)$, where\n$H_\\mu^+=\\{(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k):\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0\\}$ and\n$d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))$; at $\\mu=0$ the\nmeasure-zero apex $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted. These are the shell conventions\nof [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]].\n\nFor a smooth real classical scalar $\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$\nwith the canonical free-field length degree $-1$, adopt\n$$\\frac{S_\\phi}{\\hbar}=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi_{\\mathrm n}\\partial_b\\phi_{\\mathrm n}+\\mu^2\\phi_{\\mathrm n}^2\\right)\\,d^4x.$$\nFor a smooth commuting classical Dirac spinor\n$\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ with canonical free-field length\ndegree $-3/2$, put $\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=\\psi_{\\mathrm n}^\\dagger\\beta$ and\nuse the Clifford convention\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Adopt\n$$\\frac{S_D}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe smooth classical variables in these functionals commute; no Grassmann\nalgebra or path-integral construction is being adopted. These formulas specify\nlocal action densities: for arbitrary smooth global fields the total\nintegrals need not converge. Only compactly supported variations are used;\nthe action difference under such a variation is an integral over a compact\nset and is finite for smooth fields. With these variations on the fixed\nbackground, the intended Euler–Lagrange equations are\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0$, conditional on the exact\nmathematical supplier recorded as [L1] below.\n\nAs the physical interpretation of this selected free model, assign the real\nscalar species to the bosonic symmetric Fock representation and assign the\nDirac field to the particle/antiparticle CAR representation on\n$\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. Choose the zero-particle Fock\nvectors as the scalar and Dirac vacuum states. This is an adopted species and\nstatistics assignment. The CCR/CAR and operator properties of the specified\nrepresentations are mathematical model consequences; they do not prove a\ngeneral spin-statistics theorem or establish that nature realizes this model.\n\nThe scalar construction is the Schwartz-smeared field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, with the future-shell phase\n$p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, exactly as in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. The Dirac construction is the bounded\nspacetime-smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], using the positive and negative\nprojections of the specified free Dirac matrix. In that carrier,\n$\\mathcal H_D=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$, and\n$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=(I_4\\pm h_D(\\mathbf k)/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))/2$; the\nparticle and antiparticle one-particle spaces are the closed ranges\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. These carriers state their\nmathematical hypotheses and construction domains; their draft status is not an\nindependent physics or proof approval.\n\nTong's lecture notes and Srednicki's book draft provide independent\npresentations of the scalar and Dirac free-field formulations. Their formal\nmode notation supplies context for this adoption, not a proof of the\noperator-domain claims and not evidence that the adopted model describes\nnature.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Adopted model assumption, not a mathematical proof or empirical confirmation.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
          "carrier": "items/post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement.md",
          "status": "Current draft postulate inspected; no current Step 1 receipt exists and the QFT-C01 page edge remains open.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Adopt the following physical reading only for an explicitly selected model\nwhose mathematical data have actually been constructed. Let $\\mathscr H$ be a\nnonzero complex Hilbert space, let $\\rho_t$ be the model's density operator at\na time $t$ at which a measurement is made, and let\n$\\{M_{a,r}:r=1,\\ldots,q_a\\}_{a=1}^m$ be a supplied normalized finite bounded\nKraus instrument on $\\mathscr H$ in the exact sense of\n[[def-qft-finite-bounded-kraus-instrument]]. Assume the Axiom of Countable\nChoice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ when using the normalization result of\n[[lem-qft-finite-instrument-normalization]]. Its hypotheses include that\n$\\rho_t$ is a density operator and that the instrument really satisfies\n$\\sum_{a,r}M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}=I_{\\mathscr H}$; the supplier's result is a draft\nwith a complete conditional proof, not an independent approval or an\napparatus-realization theorem.\n\nPut\n\n$$E_a:=\\sum_rM_{a,r}^*M_{a,r},\\qquad\n\\mathcal J_a(X):=\\sum_rM_{a,r}XM_{a,r}^*.$$\n\nAs the **Born interpretation postulate** for this selected model, read\n\n$$p_a(t):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\mathcal J_a(\\rho_t))\n=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_tE_a)$$\n\nas the model probability for outcome $a$. The mathematical supplier proves\n$p_a(t)\\in[0,1]$ and $\\sum_{a=1}^mp_a(t)=1$ under the hypotheses just\nlisted. When $p_a(t)>0$, adopt\n$\\mathcal J_a(\\rho_t)/p_a(t)$ as the model's conditional state after outcome\n$a$; at zero probability no normalized update is assigned. These are\nmodel-level probability assignments, not observations.\n\nIf the selected construction supplies a self-adjoint observable in the sense\nof [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]] and its\nspectral projection-valued measure as in\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]], a declared finite Borel partition may be\nused only after its projections have been constructed and verified as a\nfinite projective instrument. In that case the projections are the single\nKraus operators for their outcomes. A supplied bounded effect is read as an\noutcome effect only as part of a supplied normalized instrument; this item\ndoes not construct square roots, a spectral measure, or an instrument from an\narbitrary formal observable.\n\nIf a model has actually constructed a self-adjoint Hamiltonian $\\widehat H$\nand its unitary evolution, adopt the Schrödinger convention\n$U(t)=\\exp(-it\\widehat H/\\hbar)$ and\n$\\rho_t=U(t)\\rho_0U(t)^*$ when that construction establishes that $\\rho_t$\nis a density operator. In the Heisenberg representation use\n$O_H(t)=U(t)^*O\\,U(t)$. This postulate neither constructs the Hamiltonian,\nunitary group, state, field representation, nor their domains.\n\nThe model declaration identifies the selected species, field normalization,\nregulator, preparation and measurement data using\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. It does not assert that every classical action\nhas a quantization, that every formal state is positive, or that any cutoff\ncan be removed. In particular it supplies no interacting continuum\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space, scattering operator, asymptotic\ncompleteness, LSZ limit, or nonperturbative convergence. A formal coefficient\nis not an exact probability without an additional controlled approximation\nmap and a constructed model. Agreement with reported data would test the\nselected approximation and empirical inputs; it would not prove this\npostulate or the model. No reported data are asserted here.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Adopted measurement interpretation, conditional on the supplied state and instrument.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-free-field-observables",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-free-field-observables.md",
          "status": "Current draft definition inspected; no current Step 1 receipt exists. The charged-number claim is explicitly conditional and is not used.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the fields, Fock spaces, vacua, coordinate conventions, and natural/SI\nnormalizations adopted in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. For an open spacetime\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, a scalar test is a real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)$ and a Dirac test is a\n$F\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$. These are compactly supported smooth test\nfunctions on the fixed inertial Minkowski chart.\n\nFor scalar tests use the bounded Weyl operators\n$$W_\\mu(f)=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere $K_\\mu$ is the future-shell restriction and\n$W_{\\rm Segal}$ is the unitary generated by the closed Segal field, with the\nnormalization and phase convention of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the scalar bounded observable algebra\n$\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ to be the norm closure of the unital $*$-algebra generated\nby these $W_\\mu(f)$. The real smeared field is symmetric on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its self-adjoint closure has the exact multiplier\ndomain supplied by [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] and\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]].\n\nFor Dirac tests, $\\Psi(F)$ is the bounded smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]]. Define $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ as the norm\nclosure of the unital algebra generated by products containing an even number\nof these field operators and their adjoints, with all test supports in $O$.\n“Even” here means even field parity, or equivalently an even number of odd\nCAR field generators in each product. The even algebra is the selected local\nDirac observable algebra; an odd charged field is not included as a commuting\nlocal observable.\n\nThe selected vacuum states on bounded operators are the vector states\n$\\omega_s(A)=\\langle\\Omega_s,A\\Omega_s\\rangle$ and\n$\\omega_D(B)=\\langle\\Omega_D,B\\Omega_D\\rangle$. For the scalar Fock space,\ndefine the total number, Hamiltonian, and translation momentum components by\n$$N_s=d\\Gamma_s(1),\\qquad H_s=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma_s(\\omega_\\mu),\\qquad P_{s,j}=\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma_s(-k_j).$$\nHere $d\\Gamma_s$ acts on the $n$-particle sector by summing the displayed\none-particle multiplier over its $n$ arguments. Their maximal domains are\nthe square-integrability domains of those sector sums; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThe sign in $P_{s,j}$ matches the field translation phase\n$\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$. For the Dirac Fock space, let $H$\nand $P_j$ be the mathematical translation generators of\n[[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. Their one-particle\nmultipliers are respectively $(\\omega_\\mu,\\mathbf k)$ for particles and\n$(\\omega_\\mu,-\\mathbf k)$ for antiparticles, and the sector multiplier is\ntheir sum. Define the physical Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum by\n$H_D=\\hbar cH$ and $P_{D,j}=\\hbar P_j$, using the positive SI constants from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Their exact domains are unchanged by these\nnonzero scale factors; their multipliers have units joules and momentum,\nrespectively.\n\nFor any of these self-adjoint operators $A$, a normalized state\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$.\nThis requires the second spectral moment but does not require\n$\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. Products of unbounded operators use their composition\ndomains.\n\nIf a signed charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in coulombs is supplied, the intended\nDirac charge-number operator is $Q_{\\rm SI}=q_{\\rm SI}(N_p-N_{\\bar p})$.\nHere $\\Pi_p$ and $\\Pi_{\\bar p}$ are the orthogonal projections onto\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$; on the $n$-particle sector,\n$N_p$ and $N_{\\bar p}$ are the sums of the corresponding projections over\nthe $n$ tensor factors. Its candidate maximal direct-sum domain is\n$$D(Q_{\\rm SI})=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left\\|q_{\\rm SI}\\sum_{r=1}^n(\\Pi_p-\\Pi_{\\bar p})^{(r)}\\Psi_n\\right\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThis is a candidate definition only; no self-adjointness or proved-domain\nclaim is asserted here.\n\nMeasurements in this model are assigned only when a state and a normalized\nfinite bounded Kraus instrument have actually been constructed. For such an\ninstrument, the adopted outcome probabilities and positive-probability\nconditional states are exactly those of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]. They are model probabilities,\nnot observed outcomes. The field definitions are test-smeared: neither\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$ nor $\\Psi(x)$ is a pointwise operator supplied here, and the\nsmearing results do not define coincident products or a diagonal restriction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition relies on draft mathematical field/domain suppliers.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F4; proof steps 1.1, 1.3."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-free-field-observables",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-free-field-observables.md",
          "status": "Current draft definition inspected; no current Step 1 receipt exists. The charged-number claim is explicitly conditional and is not used.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use the fields, Fock spaces, vacua, coordinate conventions, and natural/SI\nnormalizations adopted in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. For an open spacetime\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, a scalar test is a real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)$ and a Dirac test is a\n$F\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$. These are compactly supported smooth test\nfunctions on the fixed inertial Minkowski chart.\n\nFor scalar tests use the bounded Weyl operators\n$$W_\\mu(f)=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere $K_\\mu$ is the future-shell restriction and\n$W_{\\rm Segal}$ is the unitary generated by the closed Segal field, with the\nnormalization and phase convention of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the scalar bounded observable algebra\n$\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ to be the norm closure of the unital $*$-algebra generated\nby these $W_\\mu(f)$. The real smeared field is symmetric on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its self-adjoint closure has the exact multiplier\ndomain supplied by [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] and\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]].\n\nFor Dirac tests, $\\Psi(F)$ is the bounded smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]]. Define $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ as the norm\nclosure of the unital algebra generated by products containing an even number\nof these field operators and their adjoints, with all test supports in $O$.\n“Even” here means even field parity, or equivalently an even number of odd\nCAR field generators in each product. The even algebra is the selected local\nDirac observable algebra; an odd charged field is not included as a commuting\nlocal observable.\n\nThe selected vacuum states on bounded operators are the vector states\n$\\omega_s(A)=\\langle\\Omega_s,A\\Omega_s\\rangle$ and\n$\\omega_D(B)=\\langle\\Omega_D,B\\Omega_D\\rangle$. For the scalar Fock space,\ndefine the total number, Hamiltonian, and translation momentum components by\n$$N_s=d\\Gamma_s(1),\\qquad H_s=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma_s(\\omega_\\mu),\\qquad P_{s,j}=\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma_s(-k_j).$$\nHere $d\\Gamma_s$ acts on the $n$-particle sector by summing the displayed\none-particle multiplier over its $n$ arguments. Their maximal domains are\nthe square-integrability domains of those sector sums; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThe sign in $P_{s,j}$ matches the field translation phase\n$\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$. For the Dirac Fock space, let $H$\nand $P_j$ be the mathematical translation generators of\n[[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. Their one-particle\nmultipliers are respectively $(\\omega_\\mu,\\mathbf k)$ for particles and\n$(\\omega_\\mu,-\\mathbf k)$ for antiparticles, and the sector multiplier is\ntheir sum. Define the physical Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum by\n$H_D=\\hbar cH$ and $P_{D,j}=\\hbar P_j$, using the positive SI constants from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Their exact domains are unchanged by these\nnonzero scale factors; their multipliers have units joules and momentum,\nrespectively.\n\nFor any of these self-adjoint operators $A$, a normalized state\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$.\nThis requires the second spectral moment but does not require\n$\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. Products of unbounded operators use their composition\ndomains.\n\nIf a signed charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in coulombs is supplied, the intended\nDirac charge-number operator is $Q_{\\rm SI}=q_{\\rm SI}(N_p-N_{\\bar p})$.\nHere $\\Pi_p$ and $\\Pi_{\\bar p}$ are the orthogonal projections onto\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$; on the $n$-particle sector,\n$N_p$ and $N_{\\bar p}$ are the sums of the corresponding projections over\nthe $n$ tensor factors. Its candidate maximal direct-sum domain is\n$$D(Q_{\\rm SI})=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left\\|q_{\\rm SI}\\sum_{r=1}^n(\\Pi_p-\\Pi_{\\bar p})^{(r)}\\Psi_n\\right\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThis is a candidate definition only; no self-adjointness or proved-domain\nclaim is asserted here.\n\nMeasurements in this model are assigned only when a state and a normalized\nfinite bounded Kraus instrument have actually been constructed. For such an\ninstrument, the adopted outcome probabilities and positive-probability\nconditional states are exactly those of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]. They are model probabilities,\nnot observed outcomes. The field definitions are test-smeared: neither\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$ nor $\\Psi(x)$ is a pointwise operator supplied here, and the\nsmearing results do not define coincident products or a diagonal restriction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition relies on draft mathematical field/domain suppliers.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F5; proof steps 2.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-domain-observables.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; its Step 1 record is labelled ready, but the current itemHash check reports “Item or dependency changed; record current readiness.” Its dependency list matches current frontmatter; readiness is stale.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use the scalar and Dirac free-field constructions of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]] with their current draft hypotheses, including the Dirac projection and CAR inputs in [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For a real scalar test $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)$ is self-adjoint on the exact domain given by [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. On scalar bosonic Fock space, $N_s=d\\Gamma(1)$, $H_s=d\\Gamma(\\omega_\\mu)$, and $P_{s,j}=d\\Gamma(-k_j)$ are the maximal real multipliers of [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nand the Hamiltonian and momentum domains are exactly the square-integrability domains of the sums of their one-particle multipliers.\n\nOn the specified Dirac Fock space, spacetime-smeared fields $\\Psi(F)$ are bounded, while the translation generators $H_D,P_{D,j}$ have the exact maximal domains and joint PVM stated in [[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. For any one of these self-adjoint real-multiplier operators $A$, a normalized $\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has finite mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and finite variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2.$$\nMembership in $D(A)$ suffices for finite variance and does not require $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. A product $AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$, as specified in [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]].\n\nFor the scalar field, smooth spacetime tests approximating a point mass produce vacuum vectors with norms diverging to infinity. For the equal-time Dirac CAR field, spatial tests approximating a point mass produce bounded smeared operators whose operator norms diverge. Thus neither construction gives a point-field value in the indicated norm topology. The supplied free-field results define only smeared fields and do not define coincident diagonal products.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional proof inspected. QFT-MF01/MF02/MF03 mathematical dependencies remain drafts and are used conditionally; do not treat the stale receipt as current scaffold readiness.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F6; proof steps 1.2."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-domain-observables",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-domain-observables.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; its Step 1 record is labelled ready, but the current itemHash check reports “Item or dependency changed; record current readiness.” Its dependency list matches current frontmatter; readiness is stale.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use the scalar and Dirac free-field constructions of [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] and [[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]] with their current draft hypotheses, including the Dirac projection and CAR inputs in [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For a real scalar test $f\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$, $\\overline{\\Phi_\\mu(f)}=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)$ is self-adjoint on the exact domain given by [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. On scalar bosonic Fock space, $N_s=d\\Gamma(1)$, $H_s=d\\Gamma(\\omega_\\mu)$, and $P_{s,j}=d\\Gamma(-k_j)$ are the maximal real multipliers of [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nand the Hamiltonian and momentum domains are exactly the square-integrability domains of the sums of their one-particle multipliers.\n\nOn the specified Dirac Fock space, spacetime-smeared fields $\\Psi(F)$ are bounded, while the translation generators $H_D,P_{D,j}$ have the exact maximal domains and joint PVM stated in [[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. For any one of these self-adjoint real-multiplier operators $A$, a normalized $\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has finite mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and finite variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2.$$\nMembership in $D(A)$ suffices for finite variance and does not require $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. A product $AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$, as specified in [[lem-nrqm-uncertainty-schrodinger-robertson-anticommutator-bound]].\n\nFor the scalar field, smooth spacetime tests approximating a point mass produce vacuum vectors with norms diverging to infinity. For the equal-time Dirac CAR field, spatial tests approximating a point mass produce bounded smeared operators whose operator norms diverge. Thus neither construction gives a point-field value in the indicated norm topology. The supplied free-field results define only smeared fields and do not define coincident diagonal products.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Its current proof constructs normalized smooth bump approximate identities and proves divergence for those explicit profiles; it does not directly establish arbitrary nonnegative Schwartz profiles. This consumer proves the stated profiles locally.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F7 records the narrower supplier proof scope; steps 1.5–1.6 establish this item’s profiles directly."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-scalar-local-weyl-algebra.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].\nLet $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ be open. For real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O)$ define\n$$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere the Segal normalization is exactly\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, as in [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]. This scale makes the generator\nof $t\\mapsto W_\\mu(tf)$ equal to the real scalar field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the unital norm-closed operator\nalgebra\n$$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O)= \\overline{\\operatorname{alg}}^{\\|\\cdot\\|} \\{I,W_\\mu(f),W_\\mu(f)^*:f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)\\}.$$\nThen $O_1\\subseteq O_2$ implies\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)\\subseteq\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. If every difference\n$x-y$, with $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, is spacelike, then every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_1)$ commutes with every element of\n$\\mathfrak A_\\mu(O_2)$. These are algebraic properties of the constructed\nrepresentation; they are not empirical signaling or measurement claims.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional proof inspected; upstream field and scalar causal inputs remain open.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 is used in step 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01/MF02 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional proof inspected; upstream causal and projection inputs remain open.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F9; proof steps 1.4."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01/MF02 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional proof inspected; upstream causal and projection inputs remain open.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F10; proof steps 1.3."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "Read-only mathematical carrier inspected; this excerpt records the exact theorem interface and is not independent approval.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Mathematical convergence theorem used at its stated hypotheses.",
          "consumer_use": "F11 is used in step 1.4"
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The adopted physical systems are exactly the two selected Fock\nrepresentations in [F2], and [F1] fixes the spacetime and unit convention.\nThe test spaces and meanings of $\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ and $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ are\nthose defined in [F4]; thus all fields used here are smeared and all Dirac\nlocal observables are even products.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Apply [F6] to the listed real scalar smearings and sector-sum generators.\nIt supplies their exact maximal domains and, for every normalized\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$, finite $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and finite variance.\nThe same statement gives the product-domain condition, so no claim about\n$D(A^2)$ or an everywhere-defined product follows.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "The region hypotheses in the Statement are exactly the spacelike-support\nhypotheses of [F8] and [F10]. The first gives commutativity of the scalar\nWeyl algebras; the second gives commutativity of the bounded even Dirac local\nalgebras. If either region is empty, its test space is $\\{0\\}$ and\nits unital generated algebra is $\\mathbb C I$, which commutes with the other\nalgebra as well.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F8",
            "F10",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "claim": "Let $(\\varepsilon_n)$ be any positive sequence tending to zero. For Schwartz $g$, the scalar change of variables and dominated convergence [F11] give $\\int\\rho_{\\varepsilon_n,x_0}g\\to g(x_0)$. The same argument for spatial Schwartz spinors gives convergence of the Dirac test pairing to $z^\\dagger h(x_0)$. Since the sequence was arbitrary, both families converge to their stated point-evaluation functionals.",
          "step": "1.4",
          "inputs": [
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-5",
          "step": "1.5",
          "claim": "For any positive width sequence tending to zero, the Fourier transform of the nonnegative normalized scalar profile satisfies $|\\widehat\\rho|\\le1$ and $\\widehat\\rho(\\xi)\\to1$ by [F11]. The shell restriction therefore converges pointwise in modulus to one. On each finite annulus, dominated convergence gives convergence of its squared norm to the shell measure of that annulus. The radial shell density is at least $r/(4\\sqrt2\\pi^2)$ for $r\\ge\\max(1\\,\\mathrm m^{-1},\\mu)$, so these annular measures are unbounded as the outer radius increases. Hence the full shell norm diverges. The scalar field formula and vacuum in [F2] identify this with the vacuum-vector norm; arbitrariness of the width sequence proves divergence as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F11",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-6",
          "step": "1.6",
          "claim": "The spatial change of variables gives $\\|u_{\\epsilon,x_0}\\|_2=\\epsilon^{-3/2}\\|\\sigma\\|_2\\to\\infty$. For the bounded equal-time field $A_\\epsilon=\\Psi_t(u_{\\epsilon,x_0})$, the CAR give $A_\\epsilon A_\\epsilon^*+A_\\epsilon^*A_\\epsilon=\\|u_{\\epsilon,x_0}\\|_2^2I$. The operator norm definition, adjoint identity, triangle inequality and submultiplicativity imply $\\|u_{\\epsilon,x_0}\\|_2^2\\le2\\|A_\\epsilon\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2$, so the operator norms diverge.",
          "inputs": [
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The selected definition [F5] supplies no other point field or diagonal product. The norm obstructions in steps 1.5 and 1.6 are specific to the displayed representations and topologies.",
