          }
        ]
      },
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor a spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, with each component in\n$\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and the Euclidean norm, define\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}$, with\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here $\\omega_\\mu$ is the temporal wave number, not the\nSI angular frequency; the corresponding SI angular frequency is\n$c\\omega_\\mu$. Energy and momentum are $E=\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ in joules and\n$\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With\n$p=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4$, the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nFor a selected local action represented on a chart region\n$\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ by\n$$\\frac{S}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathcal U}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}(x)\\,d^4x,\\quad d^4x:=dx^0dx^1dx^2dx^3,$$\nthe natural Lagrangian density $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ has unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-4}$. Since $x^0=ct$, the corresponding SI Lagrangian density is\n$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\hbar c\\,\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ and has unit\n$\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. This unit conversion alone does not identify a\nLagrangian density with the model's Hamiltonian energy density.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nuse the formal field symbols $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ and $\\psi_{\\rm n}$ in the selected\nscalar and Dirac kinetic terms, assigning them length degrees $-1$ and\n$-3/2$, respectively. These are the dimensions fixed by those free kinetic\nterms, not exact scaling dimensions of interacting fields: anomalous\ndimensions, when present, are separate quantities. These symbols do not\nassert pointwise field maps or operator fields; a model supplies its field\nspace and regularity. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nIn a supplied four-component spinor representation, let\n$\\gamma^a\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathbb C^4)$, $a=0,1,2,3$, be\ndimensionless linear maps satisfying\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}:=\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a\n=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$, where $I_4$ is the identity map. Set $\\beta:=\\gamma^0$\nand $\\alpha^i:=\\beta\\gamma^i$; then $\\beta^2=I_4$ and\n$\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$. For a supplied four-component spinor $\\psi$ write\n$\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. The effective Clifford metric is $-\\eta$.\nA supplied free Dirac model may use\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$ for its model-supplied spinor field\n$\\psi$; that model supplies its field domain and regularity. The phase and\nClifford choices are conventions; this item proves no representation or field\nequation.\n\nIf a selected model supplies a complex Hilbert space $\\mathscr H$ as in\n[[def-hilbert-space]], a complex-linear Hamiltonian operator\n$H:D(H)\\to\\mathscr H$ with model-specified linear domain $D(H)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$\nand SI energy unit joules, and an evolution family $U(t):\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$\nfor $t\\in I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ in seconds, the convention is\n$U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$. Each $U(t)$ is a surjective complex-linear map\nsatisfying $\\langle U(t)\\xi,U(t)\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,\\zeta\\rangle$\nfor all $\\xi,\\zeta\\in\\mathscr H$; write $U(t)^*:=U(t)^{-1}$. The exponent\nis dimensionless because $\\hbar$ has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. For any\nmodel-supplied complex-linear operator $O:D(O)\\to\\mathscr H$, with specified\nlinear domain $D(O)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$, define its Heisenberg conjugate by\n$$O_H(t):=U(t)^*O\\,U(t),\\quad D(O_H(t)):=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathscr H:U(t)\\xi\\in D(O)\\}.$$\nThe model supplies these domains and the evolution; this dictionary constructs\nno Hamiltonian, evolution family, or field representation. Where a free-field\nmodule uses an inner product linear in its second variable, convert to the\nrepository's first-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction. It\nasserts only matrix-element continuity and the stated linear maps; all further\noperator properties, locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior must be supplied by a constructed\nmodel. Test-function units can be chosen so a specified smeared observable is\ndimensionless; there is no universal test-function unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-qft-free-field-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Fix four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, an inertial length-coordinate chart\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, $x^0=ct$, and coordinates measured in metres, as specified\nby [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Write\n$\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. The mass-shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same unit. For a\nmassive species, $\\mu=m_{\\mathrm{rest,SI}}c/\\hbar$; $\\mu=0$ is the separately\nadmitted massless branch of the mathematical free-field construction. Thus\n$\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ is physical energy and $\\hbar\\mathbf k$ is physical\nmomentum. The natural-unit action is written $S/\\hbar$.\n\nThe scalar one-particle space is\n$\\mathcal H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C)$, where\n$H_\\mu^+=\\{(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k):\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0\\}$ and\n$d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))$; at $\\mu=0$ the\nmeasure-zero apex $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted. These are the shell conventions\nof [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]].\n\nFor a smooth real classical scalar $\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$\nwith the canonical free-field length degree $-1$, adopt\n$$\\frac{S_\\phi}{\\hbar}=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi_{\\mathrm n}\\partial_b\\phi_{\\mathrm n}+\\mu^2\\phi_{\\mathrm n}^2\\right)\\,d^4x.$$\nFor a smooth commuting classical Dirac spinor\n$\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ with canonical free-field length\ndegree $-3/2$, put $\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=\\psi_{\\mathrm n}^\\dagger\\beta$ and\nuse the Clifford convention\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Adopt\n$$\\frac{S_D}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe smooth classical variables in these functionals commute; no Grassmann\nalgebra or path-integral construction is being adopted. These formulas specify\nlocal action densities: for arbitrary smooth global fields the total\nintegrals need not converge. Only compactly supported variations are used;\nthe action difference under such a variation is an integral over a compact\nset and is finite for smooth fields. With these variations on the fixed\nbackground, the intended Euler–Lagrange equations are\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0$, conditional on the exact\nmathematical supplier recorded as [L1] below.\n\nAs the physical interpretation of this selected free model, assign the real\nscalar species to the bosonic symmetric Fock representation and assign the\nDirac field to the particle/antiparticle CAR representation on\n$\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. Choose the zero-particle Fock\nvectors as the scalar and Dirac vacuum states. This is an adopted species and\nstatistics assignment. The CCR/CAR and operator properties of the specified\nrepresentations are mathematical model consequences; they do not prove a\ngeneral spin-statistics theorem or establish that nature realizes this model.\n\nThe scalar construction is the Schwartz-smeared field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, with the future-shell phase\n$p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, exactly as in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. The Dirac construction is the bounded\nspacetime-smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], using the positive and negative\nprojections of the specified free Dirac matrix. In that carrier,\n$\\mathcal H_D=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$, and\n$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=(I_4\\pm h_D(\\mathbf k)/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))/2$; the\nparticle and antiparticle one-particle spaces are the closed ranges\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. These carriers state their\nmathematical hypotheses and construction domains; their draft status is not an\nindependent physics or proof approval.\n\nTong's lecture notes and Srednicki's book draft provide independent\npresentations of the scalar and Dirac free-field formulations. Their formal\nmode notation supplies context for this adoption, not a proof of the\noperator-domain claims and not evidence that the adopted model describes\nnature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-action-equations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler–Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\npointwise restriction\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x$$\nof [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] maps $\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ continuously\nand linearly into $H_\\mu$. It also annihilates the Klein–Gordon test operator:\n$$K_\\mu\\bigl((\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2)f\\bigr)=0 \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe coordinates are the fixed length coordinates of that definition and\n$\\mu$ has inverse-length units.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. Define\n$$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4):=\\{F=(F_0,F_1,F_2,F_3):F_a\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)\\text{ for }a=0,1,2,3\\},$$\nwith the finite-product topology generated by the scalar Schwartz seminorms on each component; $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and its seminorms are those of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. For $F$ in this space, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. Define\n$$\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4):=\\{F=(F_0,F_1,F_2,F_3):F_a\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)\\text{ for }a=0,1,2,3\\},$$\nwith the finite-product topology generated by the scalar Schwartz seminorms on each component; $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ and its seminorms are those of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. For $F$ in this space, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-countable-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-countable-choice.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematical carrier inspected; this excerpt records the exact assumption and is not independent approval.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition supplies vocabulary; no theorem proof is claimed.",
          "consumer_use": "A1 is used in step 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-framework-units.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; no current Step 1 receipt was found for batch 40, whose owner status requires repair/recertification.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ put\n$\\omega(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here\n$\\omega$ is the temporal wave number, not the SI angular frequency; the\ncorresponding SI angular frequency is $c\\omega$. Energy and momentum are\n$E=\\hbar c\\omega$ in joules and $\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in\n$\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$ the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nThe natural action is $S/\\hbar$. Its canonical density in four dimensions has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-4}$; the corresponding physical energy density is\n$\\hbar c$ times that density and has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nwrite $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ for a scalar field with length degree $-1$ and\n$\\psi_{\\rm n}$ for a Dirac field with length degree $-3/2$. These are the\ndimensions fixed by the selected free kinetic terms, not exact scaling\ndimensions of interacting fields: anomalous dimensions, when present, are\nseparate quantities. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad\nf^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nThe Clifford convention is $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$,\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}$, so the effective Clifford metric is\n$-\\eta$. In a supplied free Dirac model the displayed equation is\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$. The phase and Clifford choices are\nconventions; this item proves no Dirac representation or field equation.\nTime evolution is written $U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$ when a selected model has\nactually constructed a self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ and its unitary group; the\ncorresponding Heisenberg conjugation is $O_H(t)=U(t)^*O\\,U(t)$. No such\nHamiltonian or group is constructed here. Where a free-field module uses an\ninner product linear in its second variable, convert to the repository's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction.\nSmeared fields are not thereby bounded, symmetric, self-adjoint, or\nself-adjointly closable. Locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior are likewise supplied only by the\nrelevant constructed model modules. Test-function units can be chosen so a\nspecified smeared observable is dimensionless; there is no universal test\nfunction unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition supplies conventions only; it does not prove a field equation.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in step 1.2, step 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "post-qft-free-field-model",
          "carrier": "items/post-qft-free-field-model.md",
          "status": "Current draft postulate inspected; no current Step 1 receipt exists. Its mathematical field/action inputs are conditional.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Fix four-dimensional affine Minkowski spacetime with metric\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, an inertial length-coordinate chart\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$, $x^0=ct$, and coordinates measured in metres, as specified\nby [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Write\n$\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$. The mass-shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same unit. For a\nmassive species, $\\mu=m_{\\mathrm{rest,SI}}c/\\hbar$; $\\mu=0$ is the separately\nadmitted massless branch of the mathematical free-field construction. Thus\n$\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ is physical energy and $\\hbar\\mathbf k$ is physical\nmomentum. The natural-unit action is written $S/\\hbar$.\n\nThe scalar one-particle space is\n$\\mathcal H_\\mu=L^2(H_\\mu^+,d\\nu_\\mu;\\mathbb C)$, where\n$H_\\mu^+=\\{(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k):\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3,\\ \\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)>0\\}$ and\n$d\\nu_\\mu=d^3\\mathbf k/((2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))$; at $\\mu=0$ the\nmeasure-zero apex $\\mathbf k=0$ is omitted. These are the shell conventions\nof [[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]].\n\nFor a smooth real classical scalar $\\phi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb R$\nwith the canonical free-field length degree $-1$, adopt\n$$\\frac{S_\\phi}{\\hbar}=-\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi_{\\mathrm n}\\partial_b\\phi_{\\mathrm n}+\\mu^2\\phi_{\\mathrm n}^2\\right)\\,d^4x.$$\nFor a smooth commuting classical Dirac spinor\n$\\psi_{\\mathrm n}:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$ with canonical free-field length\ndegree $-3/2$, put $\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=\\psi_{\\mathrm n}^\\dagger\\beta$ and\nuse the Clifford convention\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Adopt\n$$\\frac{S_D}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\bar\\psi_{\\mathrm n}(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}\\,d^4x.$$\nThe smooth classical variables in these functionals commute; no Grassmann\nalgebra or path-integral construction is being adopted. These formulas specify\nlocal action densities: for arbitrary smooth global fields the total\nintegrals need not converge. Only compactly supported variations are used;\nthe action difference under such a variation is an integral over a compact\nset and is finite for smooth fields. With these variations on the fixed\nbackground, the intended Euler–Lagrange equations are\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}=0$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}=0$, conditional on the exact\nmathematical supplier recorded as [L1] below.\n\nAs the physical interpretation of this selected free model, assign the real\nscalar species to the bosonic symmetric Fock representation and assign the\nDirac field to the particle/antiparticle CAR representation on\n$\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. Choose the zero-particle Fock\nvectors as the scalar and Dirac vacuum states. This is an adopted species and\nstatistics assignment. The CCR/CAR and operator properties of the specified\nrepresentations are mathematical model consequences; they do not prove a\ngeneral spin-statistics theorem or establish that nature realizes this model.\n\nThe scalar construction is the Schwartz-smeared field\n$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f)$ on the finite-particle\ndomain, with the future-shell phase\n$p\\circ x=\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$, exactly as in\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. The Dirac construction is the bounded\nspacetime-smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], using the positive and negative\nprojections of the specified free Dirac matrix. In that carrier,\n$\\mathcal H_D=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3;\\mathbb C^4)$,\n$h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf k+\\beta\\mu$, and\n$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=(I_4\\pm h_D(\\mathbf k)/\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k))/2$; the\nparticle and antiparticle one-particle spaces are the closed ranges\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$. These carriers state their\nmathematical hypotheses and construction domains; their draft status is not an\nindependent physics or proof approval.\n\nTong's lecture notes and Srednicki's book draft provide independent\npresentations of the scalar and Dirac free-field formulations. Their formal\nmode notation supplies context for this adoption, not a proof of the\noperator-domain claims and not evidence that the adopted model describes\nnature.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Adopted model assumption, not a mathematical proof or empirical confirmation.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step 1.1, step 1.3, step 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-action-equations",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-action-equations.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; its Step 1 ready receipt dependency list matches current frontmatter and hash. This is scaffold readiness only, not independent approval.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler–Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete compact-variation proof inspected; shell and Dirac convention inputs remain drafts and are used conditionally.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 1.1, step 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-mass-shell-test-restriction-continuity.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical carrier inspected; Step 1 ready receipt matches its current dependency list. This is scaffold readiness only, not independent approval.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. For every $\\mu\\ge0$, the\npointwise restriction\n$$K_\\mu f(p)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}f(x)e^{ip\\circ x}\\,d^4x$$\nof [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]] maps $\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ continuously\nand linearly into $H_\\mu$. It also annihilates the Klein–Gordon test operator:\n$$K_\\mu\\bigl((\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2)f\\bigr)=0 \\qquad(f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)).$$\nThe coordinates are the fixed length coordinates of that definition and\n$\\mu$ has inverse-length units.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional Fourier-restriction proof inspected.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional scalar-field construction inspected; upstream Fock and shell inputs remain open.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in step 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01/MF02 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Complete conditional proof inspected; upstream causal and projection inputs remain open.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions F6; proof steps 1.3."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; Step 1 decision is escalated through unresolved QFT-MF01/MF02 inputs.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, and use [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], [[lem-qft-dirac-projection-matrices]], and the antisymmetric Fock and CAR conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]] and [[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. For $F\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb C^4)$, define\n$$\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}F(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad u_F(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat F(t,\\mathbf k)\\,dt.$$\nThen $u_F\\in\\mathcal H_D$, continuously in the Schwartz topology, and\n$$\\Psi(F)=c(P_+u_F)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-u_F})$$\ndefines a bounded operator on $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$, antilinear in $F$. It satisfies\n$$\\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)\\}=0,\\qquad \\{\\Psi(F),\\Psi(G)^\\dagger\\}=\\langle u_F,u_G\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_D}I.$$\nThe positive and negative contraction kernels, defined as tempered distributions by their Fourier integrals, are\n$$C_+(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{-i(\\omega\\tau-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_+(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3},\\qquad C_-(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int e^{+i(\\omega\\tau+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x)}P_-(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}.$$\nTheir tested pairings give respectively $\\langle P_+u_F,P_+u_G\\rangle$ and $\\langle P_-u_F,P_-u_G\\rangle$; their sum is the anticommutator kernel $R$ below.\nIts tested free equation is $u_{(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F}=0$. For spatial Schwartz spinors $u,v$, the equal-time field $\\Psi_t(u)=c(P_+W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u)+c^\\dagger(\\overline{P_-W_D(t,\\mathbf k)\\widehat u})$ satisfies $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)^\\dagger\\}=\\int u(\\mathbf x)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x\\,I$ and $\\{\\Psi_t(u),\\Psi_t(v)\\}=0$.\n\nThe spacetime anticommutator kernel is\n$$R(\\tau,\\mathbf x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}W_D(-\\tau,\\mathbf k)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}=\\partial_\\tau\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x)I_4-i h_D(-i\\nabla)\\Delta_\\mu(\\tau,\\mathbf x),$$\nwhere $\\Delta_\\mu$ is the scalar causal commutator distribution of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. Thus the kernel is supported in the closed causal cone. Spinor tests with compact spacelike-separated supports have vanishing anticommutators with one another and with each other's adjoints. Consequently, even polynomials in the bounded smeared fields commute across spacelike-separated regions. These are mathematical properties of the specified free-field construction.",
