      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], for each compactly supported smooth real variation $h$ and\ncompactly supported smooth spinor variation $\\chi$, the first variations of\nthe displayed actions are the stated integrals against\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi_{\\mathrm n}$ and\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi_{\\mathrm n}$. The lemma proves both\nnecessity and sufficiency of the displayed pointwise equations for\nstationarity under all such variations; this step uses the commuting\nclassical spinor convention of [F2].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Since $\\Box-\\mu^2=-(\\partial_0^2-\\Delta+\\mu^2)$, [F4] and linearity\ngive $K_\\mu((\\Box-\\mu^2)f)=0$ for every Schwartz $f$. The differential\noperator has real coefficients, so the same identity holds for $\\overline f$.\nThe scalar field formula in [F5] applies $a$ and $a^\\dagger$ only to\n$K_\\mu\\overline f$ and $K_\\mu f$; both vanish for $f$ replaced by\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)f$. Hence the constructed scalar field satisfies the stated\nweak test equation.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "By [F7], the one-particle vector of\n$(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))F$ is zero. Substitution in the bounded CAR\nfield formula in [F6] makes both its creation and annihilation terms zero,\nso the corresponding smeared field is zero. From [F2],\n$$\\beta(i\\partial_0-h_D(-i\\nabla))\n=i\\beta\\partial_0+i\\beta\\alpha^j\\partial_j-\\mu\n=i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu,$$\nusing $\\beta^2=I_4$. Thus this is exactly the tested Dirac differential\nexpression in the classical equation of [F3].",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The quantum representations used in steps 1.2 and 1.3 enter only through\nthe physical adoption [F2]; steps 1.1–1.3 establish equation agreement under\nthat adoption and do not construct or derive the representation from the\nclassical action. The conclusion is therefore conditional on the selected\nfree model and carries no empirical or general quantization claim.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "given"
          ]
        }
