      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-qft-free-observable-domain-limits",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, $\\mu\\ge0$, and the selected free-field model of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]]. Use the four-dimensional affine Minkowski chart,\nsignature $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinate $x^0=ct$,\nand inverse-length parameter $\\mu$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. For an open\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, use the scalar and Dirac test functions and\nbounded local observable algebras defined in\n[[def-qft-free-field-observables]]: scalar tests are real members of\n$C_c^\\infty(O)$, Dirac tests are members of\n$C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$, and the Dirac observable algebra is generated by\neven products of bounded smeared CAR fields and their adjoints.\n\nFor a real Schwartz scalar test $f$, the closure of $\\Phi_\\mu(f)$ is\nself-adjoint on its exact Segal-field multiplier domain. The scalar number,\nHamiltonian and momentum operators and the Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum\noperators have the exact maximal sector-sum domains stated in\n[[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]]. In particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nIf $A$ is one of these self-adjoint operators and $\\Psi$ is normalized with\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$, then its model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$$\nare finite; this needs $\\Psi\\in D(A)$, not $\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. The product\n$AB\\Psi$ is defined only when $\\Psi\\in D(B)$ and $B\\Psi\\in D(A)$.\n\nIf $O_1,O_2$ are open regions and\n$\\eta_{ab}(x-y)^a(x-y)^b>0$ for every $x\\in O_1$ and $y\\in O_2$, then the\nscalar Weyl algebras of the two regions commute, and the even local Dirac\nalgebras of the two regions commute. These are algebraic properties of the\nspecified free representations.\n\nThe chosen construction supplies only test-smeared fields and does not define\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$, $\\Psi(x)$, or coincident diagonal products. Fix a point\n$x_0\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and a nonnegative Schwartz function\n$\\rho\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ with $\\int\\rho(y)\\,d^4y=1$. For each\nwidth $\\epsilon>0$ measured in metres, put\n$\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0}(x)=\\epsilon^{-4}\\rho((x-x_0)/\\epsilon)$. The rescaled\ncoordinate is dimensionless, so $\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0}$ has unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-4}$. These tests\nconverge on Schwartz tests to the point-evaluation functional\n$\\delta_{x_0}(g):=g(x_0)$, but\n$\\|\\Phi_\\mu(\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0})\\Omega\\|\\to\\infty$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$.\nFor the bounded equal-time CAR field $\\Psi_t(u)$ defined in\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], fix a spinor $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$ with\n$z^\\dagger z=1$ and a nonnegative Schwartz function\n$\\sigma\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^3)$ with\n$\\int\\sigma(y)\\,d^3y=1$, and put\n$u_{\\epsilon,x_0}(\\mathbf x)=\\epsilon^{-3}\n\\sigma((\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0)/\\epsilon)z$ with the same width $\\epsilon$.\nThe spatial rescaled coordinate is dimensionless and $u_{\\epsilon,x_0}$ has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-3}$. These tests approximate a\nspatial point evaluation $h\\mapsto z^\\dagger h(x_0)$ for each\n$h\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$. Write\n$\\mathcal F_D:=\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal H_+\\oplus\\overline{\\mathcal H_-})$\nfor the Dirac Fock space of [[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]], and define\n$\\|A\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}:=\\sup\\{\\|A\\xi\\|: \\xi\\in\\mathcal F_D,\\ \\|\\xi\\|=1\\}$\nfor each bounded linear operator $A:\\mathcal F_D\\to\\mathcal F_D$. For each\nfixed coordinate time $t$ in seconds,\n$\\|\\Psi_t(u_{\\epsilon,x_0})\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\to\\infty$. Neither\nfamily has a finite limit in its respective Fock or operator-norm topology.\nThis restriction does not rule out other distributional or renormalized\nconstructions. The Born interpretation of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]] applies only after a density\nstate and normalized finite bounded instrument have actually been supplied;\nthis item reports no apparatus or outcome.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
