      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For any Schwartz test $g$, the change of variables\n$y=(x-x_0)/\\epsilon$ gives\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\rho_{\\epsilon,x_0}(x)g(x)\\,d^4x\n=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\rho(y)g(x_0+\\epsilon y)\\,d^4y.$$\nThe integrand converges pointwise to $\\rho(y)g(x_0)$ and is dominated by\n$\\|g\\|_\\infty\\rho(y)$, which is integrable. Dominated convergence [F2]\ntherefore gives the limit $g(x_0)$, so these are point-mass approximants.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Take the explicit width sequence $\\epsilon_n=(1\\,\\mathrm m)/n$. By [F1], $\\|\\Phi_\\mu(\\rho_{\\epsilon_n,x_0})\\Omega\\|\\to\\infty$. A norm-convergent sequence in a Hilbert space has bounded norms, so this sequence cannot converge to a finite vacuum vector. Hence the claimed norm-limit field value does not exist in this representation. This refutes only the stated norm limit and does not exclude distributional or renormalized constructions.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
